{"id":242737959222082,"type":"song","name":"53rd and 3rd","artist":"Ramones","year":1976,"weight":13,"info":{"img":null},"artist_ref":{"id":196070018852501,"name":"The Ramones","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ramones_30081980_10_800.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Written by D.D. Ramone, this track is noted as one of the band's well-known songs, among the many he penned. \"Rockaway Beach, 53rd and 3rd in Chinese rocks were just a few of them.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Last Moments of - Part 3","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LP8NGDCe-GY"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Written by Dee Dee Ramone, this song is a stark account of his experiences \"turning tricks for drugs\" in a specific area of New York City. The track was included on the Ramones' self-titled debut record, offering a glimpse into the darker realities of his life. \"53rd and 3rd, written by D.D. Ramone about the days he spent turning tricks for drugs in that part of New York.\"","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Original Ramones","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j8uk7BnbXwU"}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":70591358521436,"type":"album","name":"Ramones","year":1976,"weight":83},{"id":114720912717001,"type":"album","name":"Rocket to Russia","year":1977,"weight":43},{"id":53483539887663,"type":"song","name":"Blitzkrieg Bop","year":1976,"weight":37},{"id":262221222853566,"type":"song","name":"Rockaway Beach","year":1977,"weight":25},{"id":277174555355804,"type":"album","name":"End Of The Century","year":1980,"weight":20},{"id":135819556979157,"type":"song","name":"KKK Took My Baby Away","year":1981,"weight":19},{"id":209261215340149,"type":"album","name":"Road to Ruin","year":1978,"weight":18},{"id":228023507146453,"type":"song","name":"Surfin' Bird